The error at line 104 is caused by the use of a semicolon after the closing brace of the function requestClassification, which is not allowed in Alloy 6. In Alloy 6, function definitions should not be followed by a semicolon when their body is a single expression enclosed in curly braces. The parser expects the end of the function definition after the closing brace, not a semicolon, hence the syntax error.

[FIX INTENT]:
Remove the extraneous semicolon after the closing brace of the function requestClassification.

[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:
- Step 1: Locate the function definition:
    fun requestClassification[r: Request]: one Clearance {
        max[r.targets.classification]
    };
- Step 2: Remove the semicolon at the end so the function ends as:
    fun requestClassification[r: Request]: one Clearance {
        max[r.targets.classification]
    }

[RATIONALE]:
This fix is compliant with Alloy 6's rule that function definitions with curly-brace bodies should not be terminated by a semicolon, and it is distinct from prior failed fixes which may have attempted to alter the function body or its delimiters rather than addressing the semicolon.

[LESSON]:
In Alloy 6, always omit the semicolon after the closing brace of a function definition; semicolons are only used to terminate simple (single-expression, non-brace) function bodies or statements within predicates and facts.
