[DIAGNOSIS]:
The error occurs at line 104, which is the end of the function requestClassification. The function is defined using curly braces {} to delimit its body and is followed by a semicolon ("};"). In Alloy 6, functions with bodies defined using curly braces should not be followed by a semicolon, as the closing brace is sufficient to terminate the function. Adding a semicolon after a function body enclosed in curly braces causes a syntax error because Alloy 6 interprets it as an unexpected token.

[FIX INTENT]:
Remove the extraneous semicolon after the closing brace of the function definition.

[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:
- Step 1: Go to line 104, which reads "};".
- Step 2: Delete the semicolon so that the line reads only "}".
- Step 3: Ensure the function definition now ends with a closing brace and no trailing semicolon:
      fun requestClassification[r: Request]: one Clearance {
          max[r.targets.classification]
      }

[RATIONALE]:
This fix is Alloy 6 compliant because it follows the rule that function definitions with block bodies (using curly braces) must not be terminated with a semicolon. This approach is different from failed strategies which may have tried to change the function body format or add extra parentheses; here, we simply remove the invalid token.

[LESSON]:
In Alloy 6, do not end function definitions with curly-brace bodies with a semicolon; only close with "}".
