[DIAGNOSIS]:
The syntax error at the beginning of fact InitialState is a downstream effect of an unclosed or malformed function definition earlier in the file, specifically the requestClassification function. The current definition:
fun requestClassification[r: Request]: one Clearance =
    max[{c: Clearance | c in r.targets.classification}]
uses a set comprehension inside max[] with curly braces, but Alloy 6 requires set comprehensions to be written with parentheses (not curly braces) when used in expressions, particularly within max[]. Prior fix attempts focused on semicolon placement or the use of '=' versus braces in function bodies, but did not address the use of curly braces inside max[], which is not valid Alloy 6 syntax.

[FIX INTENT]:
Rewrite the set comprehension argument to max[] in requestClassification to use parentheses instead of curly braces.

[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:
- Step 1: Locate the function definition for requestClassification (around line 59).
- Step 2: Change the argument to max[] from curly braces to parentheses, i.e., replace:
      max[{c: Clearance | c in r.targets.classification}]
  with:
      max[(c: Clearance | c in r.targets.classification)]
- Step 3: Ensure the rest of the function definition uses '=' and a single expression as already written.

[RATIONALE]:
This fix directly addresses Alloy 6's requirement that set comprehensions in expressions use parentheses, not curly braces, which previous attempts did not try; this will allow the parser to recognize the end of the function and correctly parse subsequent facts.

[LESSON]:
Always use parentheses for set comprehensions inside expressions in Alloy 6 (e.g., max[(x: T | ...)]), reserving curly braces for block-style set definitions or predicate/fact bodies.
